","text":"Because there are only n+m people, it's easy to prove the simulation requires O(("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") days."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"But in fact, the simulation takes only O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") days.(More accurately, the bound is ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + 1) * ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - 1) )"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"What happens? You can do some experiments by yourself. =) (you can suppose that only one person is happy in the beginning.)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In fact, this problem can be solved in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" be the greatest common divisor of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". If the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th person is happy, then all people with number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" satisfying "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/983ffaa9c1ff197aa8da595c5f61b67f6758c15d1a86aac7ca1192e6d603d9f2.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" will become happy some day because of this person."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So for each 0 ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, we only need to check if there exists at least one person whose number mod "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and is happy."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If it exists for all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", the answer is 'Yes', otherwise the answer is 'No'."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/515/submission/9906294"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"author's code"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/515/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"515-C Drazil and Factorial"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Conclusion first:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, we transform each digit of the original number as follows:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0, 1 -> empty"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2 -> 2"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3 -> 3"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"4 -> 322"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5 -> 5"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"6 -> 53"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7 -> 7"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"8 -> 7222"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"9 -> 7332"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then, sort all digits in decreasing order as a new number, then it will be the answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Proof:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can observe that our answer won't contain digits 4,6,8,9, because we can always transform digits 4,6,8,9 to more digits as in the conclusion, and it makes the number larger."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then, how can we make sure that the result is the largest after this transformation?"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can prove the following lemma:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For any positive integer "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", if it can be written as the form (2!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (3!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (5!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (7!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"c"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", there will be only one unique way."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose that there exists two ways to write down x in this form, we can assume that the two ways are (2!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (3!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (5!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (7!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" and (2!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (3!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (5!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" * (7!)"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"7"}],"kind":"subscript"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We find the largest "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≠ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", Then we know there exists at least one prime number whose factor is different in the two ways."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"But according to the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic, there is only one prime factorization of each integer. So we get a contradiction."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"After getting the result, we don't need to worry about other numbers being larger than ours."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Time Complexity: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/515/submission/9906310"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"author's code"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/515/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"515-D Drazil and Tiles"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Again we give conclusion first: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, view each cell as a vertex and connect two adjacent cells by an edge."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then, build a queue and push all vertices of degree 1 in it."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Finally, in each iteration, we pop a vertex from the queue until the queue is empty. If the vertex is used, go to the next iteration. Otherwise, we put a tile on the vertex and its adjacent vertex, and erase these two vertices from the graph. If it yields a new vertex with degree 1, push it into the queue."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"When the queue is empty, if there are still some cells not covered by any tiles, the answer will be \"Not unique.\""}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It's easy to understand that if we can put tiles on all cells by the above steps, the result is correct. But how about the remaining cases?"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will prove that when the degrees of all vertices are at least two, the solution is never unique."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose there is at least one solution."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"According to this solution, we can color those edges covered by tiles as black and color other edges as white."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can always find a cycle without any adjacent edges having the same colors. (I'll leave it as an exercise. You should notice that the graph is a bipartite graph first.)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then we can move the tiles from black edges to white edges."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So if there is at least one solution, there are in fact at least two solutions."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Time Complexity: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nm"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/515/submission/9906349"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"author's code"}],"kind":"li