"kind":"text","text":" into the group. Now we want to enumerate all nodes as the nearest node to the root. We denote the group of nodes generated from node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"G"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can do it in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b432c23d334e09047956d9416408ed84e7b9667078e9ccc415a7466093cfb2a5.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" using dfs only once. (if the length of every edge is 1, we can do it in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"))"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Imagine that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"G"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" will almost be as same as the union of all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"G"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" where node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a child of node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", but some nodes which are too far from node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are kicked out. Each node will be kicked out from the groups we considered at most once in the whole process. Now we want to know when it happens. We solve it as follows: When we do dfs, we reserve a stack to record which nodes we have visited and still need to come back to. Yes, it's just like the implementation of recursive functions. Then we can just use binary search to find the node in the stack that when we go back to it, the current node will be kicked out (the closest node with |"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"| ≥ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"So the time complexity of the above algorithm is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7e0c733dec371526f2b2cd8cab170767bbf5e3fb567892e797b18a4d2d37aed2.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we provide another algorithm with O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"qn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"α("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nlog"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) by union find. (Thanks "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Shik"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Shik"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" for providing this method.)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"First, sort all nodes by "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Then for each query, consider each node one by one from larger "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s to smaller "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At the beginning, set each node as a group of its own. We also need to record how many nodes each group contains."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"When handling a node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", union all groups of itself and its children. At the same time, for each node "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", we minus 1 from the record of how many nodes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s group has."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"By doing these, we can get the number of nodes "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s subtree with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" <  = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"d"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"L"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". That's exactly what we want to know in the last algorithm."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/516/submission/9906402"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"author's code"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" (implement with O("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"qn"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"α("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nlog"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"))))"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"level":4},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/problemset/problem/516/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"516-E Drazil and His Happy Friends"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Simplifying this question, suppose that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are coprime. If "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are not coprime and the gcd of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then we can divide all people into "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" groups by the values of their id mod "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and find the maximum answer between them. Obviously, If there is at least one group of friends which are all unhappy in the beginning, the answer is -1."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Now we determine the last one becoming happy, for boys and girls separately."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In fact, there's an easy way to explain this problem — finding the shortest path! View all friends as points, and add another point as the source. For all friends, we will view the distance from the source as the time becoming happy. And define two types of edges."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(1)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There is a fact: If a girl "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" become happy in time "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then the girl ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" will become happy in time "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"t"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So we can build a directed edge from point "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" with length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Similar for boys."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(2)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th boy/girlfriend is happy originally, we can connect it to the source with an edge of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". At the same time, we also connect the source to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th boy("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" for girl) with an edge of length "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". You can imagine that the same gender of friends form a cycle. (eg. the ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"-th boy is connected to the (("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1) * "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")%"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")-th boy for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from 0 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"With these two types of edges, we can find that if a friend is unhappy originally, he/she will become happy at the time value which is the length of the shortest path from the source."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The only question is that there are too many points and edges!"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We can solve this problem by considering only some \"important\" points."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Points connected by the second type of edges."}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Points connected to important points in 1., by the first type of edges."}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"And we can combine some consecutive edges of the first type to a new edge. The group of edges is the maximal edges that contain deleted points.(These deleted points always form a line)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Finally we find the maximum value of the shortest path from the source to these friends which is unhappy originally in the reduced graph."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Time complexity: "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/15451981ad32aa32906da5f5133104de7ee4839073894a27aa8fb012845fcc3b.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/516/submission/9972091"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"author's code"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/16468"}